────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/bg kill now actually stops the job, and /bg list reports what really happened. It's committed as 65837418, titled "Fixes #1077", so the push will close that ticket. fmt, clippy (-D warnings) and the full cargo test all pass.

What changed
• Process group: on Linux and macOS each job's sh now starts in its own process group. /bg kill sends SIGKILL to the whole group, so the shell and anything it started die together. It reuses the existing GroupKill helper from src/tools.rs; the only edit there was making it pub(crate).
• Reported state: after a kill, yoyo waits briefly for the job to finish and records its real exit status. /bg list now shows ✗ exit -9 (SIGKILL) instead of the old "could not determine" placeholder.
• Backstop: kill_on_drop(true) is set, so if the task gets aborted anyway, sh still dies. On Windows this is the only mechanism, so kill stops the shell process but not its children.
• #1063 is unchanged: the group handle is disabled the moment the shell exits on its own, so a background process it started is still left running. A new test pins that, and the existing #1063 tests are untouched and green.
• Docs: the "Known gap" line in docs/src/usage/commands.md now describes the fixed behaviour, and the ARCHITECTURE.md entry for commands_bg.rs is updated. git grep '#1077' finds no hits in docs or skills. The three hits left in src/ are code comments explaining why the code is written that way, so they stay correct after the ticket closes.

Three things that differ from the task text
• No SIGTERM step: the task asked for SIGTERM, a short wait, then SIGKILL. GroupKill only sends SIGKILL (same as the bash tool), and adding a SIGTERM step would have meant writing a second group-kill helper, which the task also ruled out. So a killed job gets no chance to clean up first.
• Jobs no longer read from the terminal (stdin is empty): a job in its own process group that tried to read the terminal would be stopped by the OS and show "running" forever. The bash tool makes the same pairing.
• Jobs stop when you quit: a job still running at /quit is now stopped too. I confirmed this on the built binary over a pseudo-terminal. I didn't check what the old binary did on quit.

Evidence
• Tests first: I wrote five tests in src/commands_bg_kill_tests.rs before the fix. The three kill tests failed (the pid was still running; the exit code was the placeholder instead of -9) and the two near-miss tests passed (a job that exits with 3 still reports 3; #1063's grandchild survives).
• Positive controls, each run as one break-run-restore command:
  • Disabling the group kill turns the grandchild and signal tests red.
  • Also removing kill_on_drop turns the shell test red as well.
  • My first control attempt only skipped the kill call and stayed green, because the handle kills the group anyway when it is dropped. The working controls disable it instead.
  • After restoring: all green, no NEUTERED markers left.
• Real binary: over a pseudo-terminal, /bg run sleep 7171 then /bg kill 1 printed Killed job [1], /bg list showed ✗ exit -9 (SIGKILL), and the sleep process was gone.
  ✓ Watch passed: `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings && cargo test`
